WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.167
(tense piano music)
(heartbeat pounding)

00:00:17.701 --> 00:00:20.270
- [Narrator] Mankind and the elements.

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:23.257
For some it's an uncomfortable bond.

00:00:23.257 --> 00:00:24.529
- When the thunderstorms came,

00:00:24.529 --> 00:00:26.006
I thought it was actually over.

00:00:26.006 --> 00:00:28.813
- [Narrator] For others,
when weather strikes,

00:00:28.813 --> 00:00:31.085
inspiration begins.

00:00:31.085 --> 00:00:35.293
- These little things are
revelations about the universe.

00:00:35.293 --> 00:00:38.330
- [Narrator] These are the
people who challenge nature,

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:41.663
seek out its limits, reveal its secrets,

00:00:43.119 --> 00:00:45.619
and embrace its awesome power.

00:00:46.482 --> 00:00:50.334
In this episode, we'll meet
an ambitious photographer

00:00:50.334 --> 00:00:52.309
determined to capture
the heart of the storm.

00:00:52.309 --> 00:00:53.952
- [Photographer] What I like
about shooting weather is

00:00:53.952 --> 00:00:56.183
you won't get the same picture twice.

00:00:56.183 --> 00:00:58.523
- [Narrator] A man exploring
the depths of the Earth

00:00:58.523 --> 00:01:01.282
who hopes to one day reach distant moons.

00:01:01.282 --> 00:01:04.256
- How could you not find satisfaction in

00:01:04.256 --> 00:01:06.149
solving those curious problems?

00:01:06.149 --> 00:01:07.672
- [Narrator] And a team of elite sailors

00:01:07.672 --> 00:01:11.154
on a mission to shatter a historic record.

00:01:11.154 --> 00:01:12.438
- This is man against ocean.

00:01:12.438 --> 00:01:14.260
This is man against the elements.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:16.761
- [Narrator] These pioneers
of the great outdoors ahead

00:01:16.761 --> 00:01:18.261
on That's Amazing.

00:01:26.789 --> 00:01:29.956
(urgent violin music)

00:01:33.005 --> 00:01:36.545
(thunder crashing)

00:01:36.545 --> 00:01:40.265
When dangerous storms
approach, most people flee.

00:01:40.265 --> 00:01:42.645
Not 21-year-old Jonas Piontek.

00:01:42.645 --> 00:01:46.342
His popular lightning photographs
result from staying put

00:01:46.342 --> 00:01:48.842
in the face of certain danger.

00:02:04.573 --> 00:02:06.288
Lake Maracaibo, Venezuela

00:02:06.288 --> 00:02:08.616
is the lightning capital of the world,

00:02:08.616 --> 00:02:11.256
where locals know the near-daily phenomena

00:02:11.256 --> 00:02:13.225
as the never-ending storm.

00:02:13.225 --> 00:02:14.184
The menacing atmosphere

00:02:14.184 --> 00:02:17.257
ignites Piontek's passion and creativity.

00:02:17.257 --> 00:02:19.741
Electrified, he snaps away,

00:02:19.741 --> 00:02:22.103
striving the capture the weather's beauty,

00:02:22.103 --> 00:02:23.770
one flash at a time.

00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:28.837
(calming electronic music)

00:02:28.837 --> 00:02:31.598
- I started with photography in 2011

00:02:31.598 --> 00:02:34.122
when I found out that my
dad has a very good camera.

00:02:34.122 --> 00:02:37.563
So I just grabbed it, went
outside, and shot everything.

00:02:37.563 --> 00:02:39.410
My interest in weather
started the same year

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:41.199
as my interest started in photography,

00:02:41.199 --> 00:02:46.128
when a tornado ravaged parts of
a town close to my home town.

00:02:46.128 --> 00:02:48.180
I started to search for pictures

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.221
and found some people out of my city

00:02:50.221 --> 00:02:52.129
that are actually hunting for storms,

00:02:52.129 --> 00:02:52.989
that are chasing those storms,

00:02:52.989 --> 00:02:54.849
and I started riding along with them,

00:02:54.849 --> 00:02:57.790
seeing and experiencing
everything they were experiencing.

00:02:57.790 --> 00:02:59.483
- Jonas is surprising young man.

00:02:59.483 --> 00:03:00.945
- That was one cool storm.

00:03:00.945 --> 00:03:02.989
I just got these bolts shooting down.

00:03:02.989 --> 00:03:04.058
- That is cool.

00:03:04.058 --> 00:03:05.992
His ability as a photographer

00:03:05.992 --> 00:03:09.086
and the way he's centered for
his age is pretty impressive.

00:03:09.086 --> 00:03:10.688
(upbeat piano music)

00:03:10.688 --> 00:03:12.656
- Being published in great magazines

00:03:12.656 --> 00:03:14.996
such as The New York Times
is a great feeling for me

00:03:14.996 --> 00:03:17.456
because I think people acknowledge my work

00:03:17.456 --> 00:03:18.596
and people like my work.

00:03:18.596 --> 00:03:19.955
What I like about shooting weather is

00:03:19.955 --> 00:03:21.900
you won't get the same picture twice.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:23.268
The weather is changing so quickly,

00:03:23.268 --> 00:03:24.888
especially when you have storms out there

00:03:24.888 --> 00:03:26.532
that you basically have one chance

00:03:26.532 --> 00:03:27.365
to get the picture you want.

00:03:27.365 --> 00:03:28.808
- Jonas has been captured

00:03:28.808 --> 00:03:31.330
by something very similar
that I was captured by

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:33.106
more than 20 years ago.

00:03:33.106 --> 00:03:36.786
It's enormous passion
for what happens here.

00:03:36.786 --> 00:03:39.665
(hip-hop beats)

00:03:39.665 --> 00:03:42.044
- [Jonas] Lake Maracaibo was
a place that not many people

00:03:42.044 --> 00:03:43.428
have traveled to yet.

00:03:43.428 --> 00:03:46.620
- Such an amazing combination
of wildlife, of culture,

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:49.956
and, of course, this spectacular
phenomenon of lightning.

00:03:49.956 --> 00:03:54.123
- [Jonas] This is probably my
favorite place on this planet.

00:03:57.745 --> 00:04:00.323
- Actually just 30
kilometers in that direction

00:04:00.323 --> 00:04:03.385
is the one point on Earth with
the most lightning strikes.

00:04:03.385 --> 00:04:04.218
These lightning strikes

00:04:04.218 --> 00:04:07.333
reach 250 per square kilometer per year.

00:04:07.333 --> 00:04:09.733
- You have to treat lightning with respect

00:04:09.733 --> 00:04:11.966
and you have to consider if a storm

00:04:11.966 --> 00:04:15.061
is far enough away to actually
get the shots you want.

00:04:15.061 --> 00:04:16.621
When the storm starts closing in

00:04:16.621 --> 00:04:19.033
you have to decide if you
should move in and take shelter,

00:04:19.033 --> 00:04:21.265
or the weather shifts
in just half a minute.

00:04:21.265 --> 00:04:24.682
(thunder booming loudly)

00:04:27.053 --> 00:04:28.602
- Generally, the storm is
not only the lightning,

00:04:28.602 --> 00:04:32.487
but it's also sometimes
hurricane force winds, tornadoes,

00:04:32.487 --> 00:04:36.925
all sorts of formations of
clouds, and very heavy rain.

00:04:36.925 --> 00:04:37.758
- [Jonas] One could describe

00:04:37.758 --> 00:04:39.899
this place turns to hell
at night, for some people.

00:04:39.899 --> 00:04:40.773
Of course, it's amazing for me,

00:04:40.773 --> 00:04:43.448
but there is so much lightning,
so much storms around.

00:04:43.448 --> 00:04:45.900
(somber piano music)

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:47.815
Lightning is the pure force of nature.

00:04:47.815 --> 00:04:49.620
It's something that is actually dangerous,

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:51.537
but beautiful together.

00:04:52.675 --> 00:04:54.404
- This lightning phenomenon here,

00:04:54.404 --> 00:04:56.384
it's just the perfect typography.

00:04:56.384 --> 00:04:59.768
It's an enormous body of
very, very warm water.

00:04:59.768 --> 00:05:03.225
Approximately 16, 17,000
kilometers of warm,

00:05:03.225 --> 00:05:05.074
very low-lying water,

00:05:05.074 --> 00:05:07.076
and it's totally surrounded by mountains.

00:05:07.076 --> 00:05:10.389
On this map here, you can
actually see by satellite

00:05:10.389 --> 00:05:11.934
the image of the storms forming here.

00:05:11.934 --> 00:05:12.857
- And you have the image

00:05:12.857 --> 00:05:15.641
almost going around the whole lake,

00:05:15.641 --> 00:05:17.730
and they will start pushing
down the cold wind at night

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:18.563
towards the lake,

00:05:18.563 --> 00:05:20.550
which creates this special
atmospheric condition

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:23.573
to actually produce extremely
intense lightning storms.

00:05:23.573 --> 00:05:25.891
(epic intense music)

00:05:25.891 --> 00:05:28.218
The biggest challenge
of shooting lightning

00:05:28.218 --> 00:05:30.466
is to decide which aperture
you're going to use.

00:05:30.466 --> 00:05:31.697
You have to do your best

00:05:31.697 --> 00:05:35.011
to actually capture this
whole experience at once,

00:05:35.011 --> 00:05:37.930
but you will never really
get the full thing captured.

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:41.589
(soft piano music)

00:05:41.589 --> 00:05:43.964
Capturing weather and
capturing storms especially

00:05:43.964 --> 00:05:44.999
is a great feeling

00:05:44.999 --> 00:05:46.917
because I can share with the
world what I'm experiencing

00:05:46.917 --> 00:05:49.257
as a traveler, as a storm chaser,

00:05:49.257 --> 00:05:52.521
and as someone who puts
himself into this danger

00:05:52.521 --> 00:05:55.149
to get the pictures and
the experience I want.

00:05:55.149 --> 00:05:58.353
It's just something that
humanity can't influence

00:05:58.353 --> 00:05:59.583
and you will feel very,

00:05:59.583 --> 00:06:02.043
very small when you’re in front of a huge storm,

00:06:02.043 --> 00:06:06.210
and that's the best feeling
for me that I can possibly get.

00:06:13.275 --> 00:06:14.108
- Aliens,

00:06:15.267 --> 00:06:17.350
jellyfish, bursting suns,

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:21.767
that all is immaterial to me.

00:06:22.612 --> 00:06:25.022
I don't really want them
to be like anything.

00:06:25.022 --> 00:06:27.689
I want them to be like nothing
else you've seen before.

00:06:27.689 --> 00:06:31.106
(mystical chiming music)

00:06:32.465 --> 00:06:33.987
My name is Anthony Howe.

00:06:33.987 --> 00:06:35.469
I'm a kinetic wind sculptor

00:06:35.469 --> 00:06:38.552
residing on Orcas Island, Washington.

00:06:47.096 --> 00:06:48.774
The unifying element to all my work

00:06:48.774 --> 00:06:52.901
is that the outdoor stuff
is all powered by the wind.

00:06:52.901 --> 00:06:56.788
The wind is attractive
because it's always there.

00:06:56.788 --> 00:06:59.528
It's not taking anything
away from anything else,

00:06:59.528 --> 00:07:02.368
and the fact that it can be
used to make a three dimensional

00:07:02.368 --> 00:07:04.868
object move is perfect for me.

00:07:06.748 --> 00:07:09.268
I don't have a gallery
and haven't had one for

00:07:09.268 --> 00:07:10.518
about 30 years.

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:15.424
My wife and I have run the
whole business ourselves

00:07:15.424 --> 00:07:18.953
without agents, or
galleries, or consultants.

00:07:18.953 --> 00:07:22.300
Most of the people that see my work,

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:24.383
see it on YouTube videos.

00:07:26.081 --> 00:07:29.129
A large scale piece will
generally start at say,

00:07:29.129 --> 00:07:32.046
45,000, 50,000 and go up to 400,000

00:07:33.856 --> 00:07:35.657
if it's a really big sculpture,

00:07:35.657 --> 00:07:39.316
but that's a kind of a piece
that takes a long time to make,

00:07:39.316 --> 00:07:43.399
and extracts a lot of
physical ability out of me.

00:07:45.628 --> 00:07:49.545
(upbeat acoustic guitar music)

00:07:57.809 --> 00:07:59.106
What I'm trying to do with the work

00:07:59.106 --> 00:08:03.106
is cause people to stop
whatever thought process

00:08:04.038 --> 00:08:06.302
they have in their head,

00:08:06.302 --> 00:08:09.743
and just for a moment, experience

00:08:09.743 --> 00:08:14.147
a different kind of reality,
maybe more meditative.

00:08:14.147 --> 00:08:16.294
They work, they take people out of their,

00:08:16.294 --> 00:08:19.557
whatever nonsense is
going on in their heads,

00:08:19.557 --> 00:08:21.538
puts them in a different place.

00:08:21.538 --> 00:08:23.674
It's a feeling you get
when you see something

00:08:23.674 --> 00:08:26.819
that is very beautiful and unusual.

00:08:26.819 --> 00:08:30.402
That's what I'm trying
to do with the work.

00:08:35.432 --> 00:08:38.599
(calming piano music)

00:08:41.652 --> 00:08:45.410
- [Narrator] In 2013,
entrepreneur Jim Clarke

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:48.576
envisioned a boat built purely for speed.

00:08:48.576 --> 00:08:51.361
A boat so light, so fast,

00:08:51.361 --> 00:08:54.578
that it would shatter
every record it attempts.

00:08:54.578 --> 00:08:55.411
In 2014,

00:08:57.579 --> 00:08:59.715
that dream became a reality

00:08:59.715 --> 00:09:02.403
with the 100 foot Monohull Camanche.

00:09:02.403 --> 00:09:04.095
- [Man] It's by almost any measure,

00:09:04.095 --> 00:09:06.076
the fastest Monohull in the world.

00:09:06.076 --> 00:09:07.828
- [Narrator] With her
revolutionary design,

00:09:07.828 --> 00:09:10.013
she has since been dominating the world

00:09:10.013 --> 00:09:11.835
of professional sailing,

00:09:11.835 --> 00:09:14.991
blowing competitors out of the water.

00:09:14.991 --> 00:09:18.189
Now skipper Ken Read is on a mission

00:09:18.189 --> 00:09:21.574
to break one of the sport's
most prestigious records,

00:09:21.574 --> 00:09:23.517
the Transatlantic Record.

00:09:23.517 --> 00:09:27.117
- It's one of the holy grails
of ocean going records.

00:09:27.117 --> 00:09:31.306
- [Narrator] A grueling journey
of 2,880 nautical miles,

00:09:31.306 --> 00:09:34.639
from New York, to the south tip England.

00:09:37.768 --> 00:09:40.659
The crew's success depends
largely on how elite navigator,

00:09:40.659 --> 00:09:43.911
Stan Honey, can predict,
harness, and adapt

00:09:43.911 --> 00:09:46.767
to the Atlantic's fickle weather patterns.

00:09:46.767 --> 00:09:50.104
If broken, this record will
secure Camanche's position

00:09:50.104 --> 00:09:52.479
as one of the most successful racing boats

00:09:52.479 --> 00:09:54.062
in sailing history.

00:10:01.132 --> 00:10:03.220
(fast paced piano music)

00:10:03.220 --> 00:10:05.115
- [Ken] The build team that we assembled

00:10:05.115 --> 00:10:06.459
are the best of the best.

00:10:06.459 --> 00:10:09.141
- There was no expense spared
in creating this thing.

00:10:09.141 --> 00:10:11.481
It's all like, carbon or titanium.

00:10:11.481 --> 00:10:13.848
- Carbon fiber sails, mast, everything,

00:10:13.848 --> 00:10:17.233
it's just making for boats
that go significantly faster

00:10:17.233 --> 00:10:18.553
than they have in the past.

00:10:18.553 --> 00:10:21.035
- Kenny Read is the skipper,

00:10:21.035 --> 00:10:22.932
and Kenny couldn't have been more clear

00:10:22.932 --> 00:10:24.684
with what they wanted to build.

00:10:24.684 --> 00:10:28.272
A boat that would be as fast as possible.

00:10:28.272 --> 00:10:31.632
A boat that was legal to
enter the major yacht races

00:10:31.632 --> 00:10:32.774
around the world,

00:10:32.774 --> 00:10:36.277
and ideally, to set the course
records for these events.

00:10:36.277 --> 00:10:38.100
(tense action music)

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:40.391
- Ultimately, what I'm pretty good at doing

00:10:40.391 --> 00:10:43.471
is getting a compatible group of all-stars

00:10:43.471 --> 00:10:44.982
from all around the globe,

00:10:44.982 --> 00:10:47.118
that can actually handle a boat like this.

00:10:47.118 --> 00:10:49.337
It's like a Formula One car,
you let it get away from you,

00:10:49.337 --> 00:10:51.337
it'll get away from you.

00:10:54.306 --> 00:10:55.554
- It is a very dangerous boat.

00:10:55.554 --> 00:10:56.945
Everybody who is on board this boat,

00:10:56.945 --> 00:10:59.754
either has America's
cup, Volvo Ocean Race,

00:10:59.754 --> 00:11:02.034
Grand Prix high end racing background.

00:11:02.034 --> 00:11:04.964
It's their career, it's their job.

00:11:04.964 --> 00:11:07.295
- The boat's a 100 foot screaming machine

00:11:07.295 --> 00:11:08.595
that has been built to

00:11:08.595 --> 00:11:10.602
break every record in
the world that we can

00:11:10.602 --> 00:11:13.565
and she's on her way to doing that.

00:11:13.565 --> 00:11:14.550
- [Narrator] Once in the water,

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:18.217
Camanche immediately
began breaking records.

00:11:19.466 --> 00:11:20.774
- We just gotta go out and sail well,

00:11:20.774 --> 00:11:22.691
and let the chips fall.

00:11:26.126 --> 00:11:26.959
I think it's fair to say,

00:11:26.959 --> 00:11:30.876
we got the fastest hundred
footer in the world.

00:11:32.021 --> 00:11:33.749
- [Narrator] With wind in their sails,

00:11:33.749 --> 00:11:35.574
the Camanche team set their sights

00:11:35.574 --> 00:11:38.214
on sailing's most prestigious prize,

00:11:38.214 --> 00:11:40.297
the Transatlantic Record.

00:11:43.817 --> 00:11:46.062
- Stan Honey is a legend in our sport.

00:11:46.062 --> 00:11:47.525
When you think of all
the great navigators,

00:11:47.525 --> 00:11:50.189
Stan is one of those guys
right at the top of the list

00:11:50.189 --> 00:11:51.413
who comes to mind.

00:11:51.413 --> 00:11:52.720
The amount of work that he puts into it

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:53.909
to make these attempts happen,

00:11:53.909 --> 00:11:55.457
without him, it wouldn't happen.

00:11:55.457 --> 00:11:58.073
- So, the Transatlantic Monohull Record

00:11:58.073 --> 00:12:02.240
is probably the most prestigious
sailing record of any.

00:12:14.018 --> 00:12:16.057
The current project
we're setting out to do

00:12:16.057 --> 00:12:18.350
is to try to set the Monohull record

00:12:18.350 --> 00:12:20.581
for the fastest transatlantic passage.

00:12:20.581 --> 00:12:22.670
That record is currently set at six days,

00:12:22.670 --> 00:12:24.420
17 hours by Mari Cha.

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:30.514
- This record is first of
all, all about weather,

00:12:30.514 --> 00:12:33.516
and it's putting the boat
in just ahead of harm's way.

00:12:33.516 --> 00:12:35.447
- [Stan] The conditions
where the boat really excels

00:12:35.447 --> 00:12:37.330
is where it's windy.

00:12:37.330 --> 00:12:39.646
- We'd been watching
the weather for months.

00:12:39.646 --> 00:12:41.663
We're looking for a front

00:12:41.663 --> 00:12:43.895
that's gonna be coming
off of North America.

00:12:43.895 --> 00:12:45.922
It doesn't have to be
a huge amount of wind.

00:12:45.922 --> 00:12:48.298
We don't need a lot of wind
to make this boat go fast.

00:12:48.298 --> 00:12:51.263
- Since 2004, there
tends to be four or five

00:12:51.263 --> 00:12:53.230
good weather windows

00:12:53.230 --> 00:12:56.758
where Camanche could have substantially

00:12:56.758 --> 00:12:57.912
improved on the record,

00:12:57.912 --> 00:13:01.229
meaning taken 18 hours or
more off of the record.

00:13:01.229 --> 00:13:04.104
What I always do, is to choose to sail,

00:13:04.104 --> 00:13:05.942
a course that's longer than a great circle

00:13:05.942 --> 00:13:08.940
because of the expected benefit.

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:11.677
You'll get there sooner
because of the predictions

00:13:11.677 --> 00:13:12.927
of the weather.

00:13:14.973 --> 00:13:16.702
- You can't take the ocean for granted.

00:13:16.702 --> 00:13:17.937
This is man against ocean.

00:13:17.937 --> 00:13:19.114
This is man against the elements.

00:13:19.114 --> 00:13:20.577
If I've learned anything,

00:13:20.577 --> 00:13:23.110
you start to just get a
little cocky in the ocean,

00:13:23.110 --> 00:13:25.110
and it'll bite you hard.

00:13:26.728 --> 00:13:30.145
(cautious pensive music)

00:13:43.624 --> 00:13:45.398
- Volcana just came at a period where

00:13:45.398 --> 00:13:47.583
he had a prior commitment
with the America's cup,

00:13:47.583 --> 00:13:50.333
but that's the nature of weather.

00:13:54.494 --> 00:13:55.736
I'd shit my pants to start with.

00:13:55.736 --> 00:13:57.986
(laughing)

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:00.668
To be able to be told that,

00:14:00.668 --> 00:14:02.528
“Hey, you're gonna be the guy in charge."

00:14:02.528 --> 00:14:05.318
And I was really, really
happy to be in that position

00:14:05.318 --> 00:14:07.135
and trusted like that.

00:14:07.135 --> 00:14:09.020
- Casey in the pit.

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:10.700
Nick Dana at the rig.

00:14:10.700 --> 00:14:13.219
- The transatlantic was a
very interesting one for us

00:14:13.219 --> 00:14:16.052
because we had three false starts.

00:14:17.156 --> 00:14:18.943
You're waiting for that exact window

00:14:18.943 --> 00:14:20.720
and you have to be ready at any moment.

00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:23.109
So, your life's pretty much on hold.

00:14:23.109 --> 00:14:25.265
At this point, we're not that confident

00:14:25.265 --> 00:14:27.633
that this was actually the one.

00:14:27.633 --> 00:14:30.164
- We are getting constant
information from Stan.

00:14:30.164 --> 00:14:34.247
- We wanna start as close
as we can to the front,

00:14:35.337 --> 00:14:37.256
but ahead of the thunderstorms,

00:14:37.256 --> 00:14:39.453
because if the thunderstorms catch us,

00:14:39.453 --> 00:14:40.569
you guys know what happens.

00:14:40.569 --> 00:14:43.856
It's a little bit of a feeling
of, “Is this gonna happen?”

00:14:43.856 --> 00:14:48.191
- It felt like a bit
like launch day for NASA.

00:14:48.191 --> 00:14:51.441
(epic inspiring music)

00:14:55.776 --> 00:14:56.978
- And then when we pushed off the dock

00:14:56.978 --> 00:14:58.715
and started motoring out,

00:14:58.715 --> 00:15:00.791
that's when it really was like, “Wow!”

00:15:00.791 --> 00:15:03.048
We're doing it, we're gonna go.

00:15:03.048 --> 00:15:06.024
(tense suspenseful music)

00:15:06.024 --> 00:15:08.902
It's real; it's happening.

00:15:08.902 --> 00:15:11.207
We positioned ourselves about a mile

00:15:11.207 --> 00:15:13.823
away from the Ambrose Light,

00:15:13.823 --> 00:15:14.974
which gives us enough runway

00:15:14.974 --> 00:15:17.974
to hit the start line at full speed.

00:15:20.330 --> 00:15:22.394
We had the sail combination
that we wanted to use,

00:15:22.394 --> 00:15:23.786
we had the perfect wind.

00:15:23.786 --> 00:15:25.953
It was time to let it rip.

00:15:26.871 --> 00:15:30.290
The acceleration doing
25 knots to 30 knots.

00:15:30.290 --> 00:15:32.830
- 20, 21, this is perfect guys.

00:15:32.830 --> 00:15:35.334
- [Stan] Doing that jump in
such a short amount of time

00:15:35.334 --> 00:15:37.206
can often put you on your butt.

00:15:37.206 --> 00:15:38.789
- Copy, 16, 58, 16.

00:15:40.458 --> 00:15:43.179
- And we come passed the
Ambrose Light fully lit up. It was…

00:15:43.179 --> 00:15:45.467
Everyone gave a huge hoot and holler

00:15:45.467 --> 00:15:49.634
as we went passed the start
line, and off we went.

00:15:51.002 --> 00:15:52.447
- [Narrator] July 22nd,
(epic adventurous music)

00:15:52.447 --> 00:15:54.582
team Camanche is off like a rocket,

00:15:54.582 --> 00:15:57.476
sailing from New York City,
toward the tip of England,

00:15:57.476 --> 00:15:59.767
determined to crush the
Transatlantic Record.

00:15:59.767 --> 00:16:01.375
- The record time that we're trying beat

00:16:01.375 --> 00:16:02.851
is six days, 18 hours,

00:16:02.851 --> 00:16:07.141
and we're hoping to take a
good 24 hours off that record.

00:16:07.141 --> 00:16:08.894
- Okay, are you getting it?

00:16:08.894 --> 00:16:10.467
- And it's pretty radical.

00:16:10.467 --> 00:16:12.483
You've got all sail area up.

00:16:12.483 --> 00:16:14.426
You got all the power
in the world you need.

00:16:14.426 --> 00:16:18.593
So you know, for a start
you don't wanna mess it up.

00:16:25.069 --> 00:16:26.101
- [Narrator] But just as they're off,

00:16:26.101 --> 00:16:28.237
things take a dangerous turn.

00:16:28.237 --> 00:16:31.201
As Camanche tangles with
a formidable thunderstorm.

00:16:31.201 --> 00:16:32.342
- The worst part of it,

00:16:32.342 --> 00:16:34.754
the most dangerous part, weather-wise,

00:16:34.754 --> 00:16:37.237
was actually the first
night and that next morning.

00:16:37.237 --> 00:16:39.038
(pensive suspenseful music)

00:16:39.038 --> 00:16:42.170
- We started ahead of the
front and the thunderstorms

00:16:42.170 --> 00:16:44.628
preceding the front came
out earlier than expected,

00:16:44.628 --> 00:16:46.795
and kind of gobbled us up.

00:16:51.797 --> 00:16:53.226
- I thought it was over.

00:16:53.226 --> 00:16:56.036
When the thunderstorms came, I
thought it was actually over.

00:16:56.036 --> 00:16:59.203
- We definitely had to sail a course

00:16:59.203 --> 00:17:00.452
that we weren't expecting to sail,

00:17:00.452 --> 00:17:01.958
and the first thing in my mind is,

00:17:01.958 --> 00:17:04.992
“Are we sailing home and
going on standby again?”

00:17:04.992 --> 00:17:07.525
- And we managed to just
wiggle our way out of it,

00:17:07.525 --> 00:17:09.385
and keep on going.

00:17:09.385 --> 00:17:10.218
(calm pensive music)

00:17:10.218 --> 00:17:11.826
Being on board the Camanche

00:17:11.826 --> 00:17:14.345
in the middle of the Atlantic
ocean is something that

00:17:14.345 --> 00:17:17.678
you realize how small you are out there.

00:17:18.963 --> 00:17:21.075
Personally, I love the silence.

00:17:21.075 --> 00:17:22.888
- You have a lot of time to
think when you're out there.

00:17:22.888 --> 00:17:24.375
The day slows down a lot.

00:17:24.375 --> 00:17:27.036
You're living every hour.

00:17:27.036 --> 00:17:29.517
- It's kind of cool to just disconnect

00:17:29.517 --> 00:17:32.745
and you're out there in the elements,

00:17:32.745 --> 00:17:36.935
the wind, and the waves,
it's nothing like it.

00:17:36.935 --> 00:17:37.852
It's crazy.

00:17:39.764 --> 00:17:42.544
Things move differently
compared to other kill boats

00:17:42.544 --> 00:17:43.840
I've sailed on.

00:17:43.840 --> 00:17:46.301
One minute you're doing 10 miles an hour,

00:17:46.301 --> 00:17:48.892
and then the next, you blink,

00:17:48.892 --> 00:17:53.059
and honestly, you're up
to 20 to 30 miles an hour.

00:17:54.224 --> 00:17:57.920
(suspenseful music)

00:17:57.920 --> 00:17:58.753
- You got 18 guys on board,

00:17:58.753 --> 00:18:00.836
and it's for five and a half days.

00:18:00.836 --> 00:18:02.732
So it's pretty tight quarters,

00:18:02.732 --> 00:18:04.376
even though it is a hundred foot boat.

00:18:04.376 --> 00:18:06.740
You're sharing bunks, sharing bowls,

00:18:06.740 --> 00:18:10.907
you're sharing water bottles
for drinking and everything.

00:18:15.123 --> 00:18:17.510
You're working the sails, you're
grinding, you're steering,

00:18:17.510 --> 00:18:20.113
you're always sort of losing,
losing, losing, losing

00:18:20.113 --> 00:18:21.613
against the sleep.

00:18:23.726 --> 00:18:25.046
- There's no place I'd rather be,

00:18:25.046 --> 00:18:28.767
even though it's a terrible
place in a lot of respects.

00:18:28.767 --> 00:18:30.314
I wish I had new boots.

00:18:30.314 --> 00:18:32.367
(laughing)

00:18:32.367 --> 00:18:33.388
Okay, guys,

00:18:33.388 --> 00:18:37.555
there's an ice contour that
we're keeping track of.

00:18:45.125 --> 00:18:49.060
- You couldn't see more than
100 feet in front of the boat.

00:18:49.060 --> 00:18:50.575
Fully engulfed in fog.

00:18:50.575 --> 00:18:51.703
You know, we're doing 20 knots,

00:18:51.703 --> 00:18:54.503
100 feet isn't enough
warning to avoid the ice.

00:18:54.503 --> 00:18:56.816
- Yeah, we're still in
the vicinity of ice.

00:18:56.816 --> 00:18:58.784
So, we have to be cautious.

00:18:58.784 --> 00:19:00.824
- It's a real nervous time for the crew,

00:19:00.824 --> 00:19:02.168
and it's hard to be here.

00:19:02.168 --> 00:19:03.751
It's hard to sleep.

00:19:07.328 --> 00:19:10.578
(deep emotional music)

00:19:12.140 --> 00:19:13.545
- We knew we were on the pace.

00:19:13.545 --> 00:19:16.212
We knew that the time was right.

00:19:17.199 --> 00:19:18.857
- But it wasn't until seeing England,

00:19:18.857 --> 00:19:21.545
and having the wind there as we got close,

00:19:21.545 --> 00:19:22.564
that it sunk in.

00:19:22.564 --> 00:19:26.064
Then it was like, “Wow, we're gonna do this.”

00:19:27.592 --> 00:19:31.592
We got very, very lucky
with our weather window.

00:19:33.387 --> 00:19:36.213
That Stan refers to it as
the postage stamp of wind

00:19:36.213 --> 00:19:37.929
to carry across.

00:19:37.929 --> 00:19:40.965
- Essentially, we had crushed the record.

00:19:40.965 --> 00:19:41.882
- Now, hey!

00:19:43.712 --> 00:19:46.066
(clapping)

00:19:46.066 --> 00:19:48.733
- Good on you mate, good on you.

00:19:56.134 --> 00:19:58.053
- This boat was built to do that.

00:19:58.053 --> 00:19:58.989
It did it.

00:19:58.989 --> 00:20:00.739
We're all very happy.

00:20:04.344 --> 00:20:05.628
- It's such a special thing

00:20:05.628 --> 00:20:08.293
to be able to break this
Transatlantic Record.

00:20:08.293 --> 00:20:10.993
Huge thanks to the weather gods
to allow us to do it as well

00:20:10.993 --> 00:20:13.895
because that's what it really took.

00:20:13.895 --> 00:20:17.395
(epic accomplished music)

00:20:22.366 --> 00:20:24.947
(curious inquisitive music)

00:20:24.947 --> 00:20:27.215
- [Man] Is there anybody
else out there besides us?

00:20:27.215 --> 00:20:31.883
Why should we be interested
in finding life elsewhere?

00:20:31.883 --> 00:20:32.716
- [Narrator] These are
the questions raised

00:20:32.716 --> 00:20:34.705
by Doctor Bill Stone.

00:20:34.705 --> 00:20:38.232
Explorer, caver, inventor
of a new class of robot

00:20:38.232 --> 00:20:39.565
called cryobots.

00:20:40.499 --> 00:20:45.228
- There are a number of moons
out there called ocean worlds.

00:20:45.228 --> 00:20:47.784
- [Narrator] Stone is building
his cryobots with NASA

00:20:47.784 --> 00:20:50.784
for unmanned exploration in space,

00:20:50.784 --> 00:20:53.124
nearly 400,000,000 miles from Earth.

00:20:53.124 --> 00:20:54.681
- There's a reasonable chance

00:20:54.681 --> 00:20:57.756
that we could find microbial
life in those oceans.

00:20:57.756 --> 00:21:01.308
Simple-celled, single-celled,
multi-celled creatures.

00:21:01.308 --> 00:21:04.308
Could that exist on other worlds?

00:21:04.308 --> 00:21:05.975
- [Narrator] The team he's
assembled spends years

00:21:05.975 --> 00:21:09.407
testing these robots in Earth's
most extreme environments.

00:21:09.407 --> 00:21:12.471
- Try to take science-fiction
and turn it into science-fact.

00:21:12.471 --> 00:21:14.328
- [Narrator] By the time
their trip is scheduled,

00:21:14.328 --> 00:21:15.972
his cryobots will be ready

00:21:15.972 --> 00:21:19.055
to search for life across the galaxy.

00:21:20.706 --> 00:21:24.289
(upbeat inquisitive music)

00:21:25.313 --> 00:21:28.241
- I was 14 when Neil
Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin

00:21:28.241 --> 00:21:29.824
landed on the moon.

00:21:33.783 --> 00:21:35.211
I wanted to be just like those guys.

00:21:35.211 --> 00:21:36.952
I wanted to be an astronaut.

00:21:36.952 --> 00:21:38.616
I wanted to go to the moon and beyond.

00:21:38.616 --> 00:21:42.098
I went through college, grad
school, and a PhD program

00:21:42.098 --> 00:21:45.769
thinking all along that I was
gonna join the astronaut corps

00:21:45.769 --> 00:21:48.733
and we were gonna go back to the moon.

00:21:48.733 --> 00:21:50.245
I was not selected.

00:21:50.245 --> 00:21:52.153
I was told I was too independent.

00:21:52.153 --> 00:21:53.388
(laughing)

00:21:53.388 --> 00:21:55.305
But that's okay because

00:21:56.523 --> 00:21:58.322
I've since gone on to do work

00:21:58.322 --> 00:22:01.276
related to private space exploration,

00:22:01.276 --> 00:22:05.359
and exploration here on
Earth in very deep caves.

00:22:06.815 --> 00:22:09.275
Some of our crews have
been to the deepest places

00:22:09.275 --> 00:22:11.987
inside this planet that
anybody has ever been,

00:22:11.987 --> 00:22:15.503
and distances so remote that
it takes more time to get there

00:22:15.503 --> 00:22:19.608
than it would to get
to the moon by rocket.

00:22:19.608 --> 00:22:22.967
So, to me, that's kind of
where I really find life.

00:22:22.967 --> 00:22:27.312
Everything else in between
is just preparing for that.

00:22:27.312 --> 00:22:31.316
(inspiring and uplifting music)

00:22:31.316 --> 00:22:33.942
I founded Stone Aerospace
about 15 years ago

00:22:33.942 --> 00:22:36.799
with the idea of putting
together a collection

00:22:36.799 --> 00:22:39.115
of very talented individuals,

00:22:39.115 --> 00:22:42.524
who could take on difficult
scientific problems

00:22:42.524 --> 00:22:44.774
and turn them into reality.

00:22:48.714 --> 00:22:49.867
A lot of the stuff that we do

00:22:49.867 --> 00:22:52.206
involves expeditionary type work

00:22:52.206 --> 00:22:54.523
to places where it's difficult to get to

00:22:54.523 --> 00:22:58.106
and requires technology
to do things there.

00:22:59.046 --> 00:23:02.963
Europa is one of the
Galilean moons of Jupiter.

00:23:03.907 --> 00:23:05.815
It is entirely covered with ice,

00:23:05.815 --> 00:23:09.982
and that ice is over an
ocean of significant depth.

00:23:17.491 --> 00:23:19.639
To me, it's a very interesting
philosophical question.

00:23:19.639 --> 00:23:21.860
You know, is there life other than Earth?

00:23:21.860 --> 00:23:25.506
And here is a chance within
our lifetime to prove it

00:23:25.506 --> 00:23:27.918
one way or another.

00:23:27.918 --> 00:23:30.307
The best place that we can
get on Earth right now,

00:23:30.307 --> 00:23:35.286
that is kind of like Europa,
is the Antarctic ice shelf.

00:23:35.286 --> 00:23:38.554
That has depths of ice
of up to 4,000 meters

00:23:38.554 --> 00:23:41.082
over sub-glacial lakes.

00:23:41.082 --> 00:23:44.095
If we could build a device
that would go through

00:23:44.095 --> 00:23:46.830
4,000 meters of ice in Antarctica,

00:23:46.830 --> 00:23:50.997
we would be paving the way
to enabling a Europa mission.

00:23:53.634 --> 00:23:55.842
A cryobot is a general term

00:23:55.842 --> 00:24:00.162
for what some people would
refer to as a melt-probe,

00:24:00.162 --> 00:24:01.912
an an ice penetrator.

00:24:03.907 --> 00:24:05.431
This is Valkyrie.

00:24:05.431 --> 00:24:08.672
This is the world's first
laser powered cryobot.

00:24:08.672 --> 00:24:10.436
This entire thing right here,

00:24:10.436 --> 00:24:13.269
is a closed cycle hot water drill.

00:24:19.999 --> 00:24:23.166
(calming piano music)

00:24:32.959 --> 00:24:35.684
The purpose of a cryobot
is to get through the ice,

00:24:35.684 --> 00:24:37.104
but once you get there, what do you do?

00:24:37.104 --> 00:24:40.069
Well, the idea is to deliver an
autonomous underwater vehicle

00:24:40.069 --> 00:24:42.319
that goes looking for life.

00:24:44.051 --> 00:24:48.218
Artemis is a hovering
autonomous underwater vehicle.

00:24:51.804 --> 00:24:55.971
It was built to test ideas
for how to explore long ranges

00:24:56.951 --> 00:24:58.368
under an ice cap,

00:25:01.223 --> 00:25:05.603
and to investigate ways in
which you would look for life

00:25:05.603 --> 00:25:07.770
in environments like that.

00:25:10.068 --> 00:25:11.675
We've been to Antarctica three times now.

00:25:11.675 --> 00:25:15.407
I've spent almost a year
there with our crews.

00:25:15.407 --> 00:25:19.150
There is a really large
suite of scientific sensors

00:25:19.150 --> 00:25:20.077
on this vehicle.

00:25:20.077 --> 00:25:22.705
Everything from five
High-Definition cameras,

00:25:22.705 --> 00:25:24.982
to water collection systems.

00:25:24.982 --> 00:25:27.740
We can measure ocean currents.

00:25:27.740 --> 00:25:30.116
- So I'm getting a line that way.

00:25:30.116 --> 00:25:32.228
- And we can tell with tracking antennas

00:25:32.228 --> 00:25:34.760
where the vehicle is
moving below the ice cap

00:25:34.760 --> 00:25:38.593
even if it's hundreds
of meters below the ice.

00:25:40.211 --> 00:25:42.385
This is the first one in
which we've had a vehicle

00:25:42.385 --> 00:25:44.747
where you drill through and ice cap

00:25:44.747 --> 00:25:47.447
and then send it off for
long distances on its own,

00:25:47.447 --> 00:25:48.408
and have it come back,

00:25:48.408 --> 00:25:51.503
and actually dock, latch,
and be automatically

00:25:51.503 --> 00:25:53.336
pulled to the surface.

00:25:55.884 --> 00:26:00.336
So, this is really a
prototype for a long-range

00:26:00.336 --> 00:26:03.565
Europa sub-ice carrier vehicle.

00:26:03.565 --> 00:26:05.147
What we would refer to as the mother ship.

00:26:05.147 --> 00:26:07.163
Something that would be able to create

00:26:07.163 --> 00:26:11.712
topographical maps of the
core of Europa, for example.

00:26:11.712 --> 00:26:14.379
It requires onboard intelligence

00:26:15.731 --> 00:26:18.661
at a level which we
have never deployed yet,

00:26:18.661 --> 00:26:22.828
not ever for example, with
Curiosity on Mars right now.

00:26:29.279 --> 00:26:32.387
So, the robots will have
to be much more independent

00:26:32.387 --> 00:26:34.139
than they are today,

00:26:34.139 --> 00:26:36.443
and they will have very specific roles

00:26:36.443 --> 00:26:38.443
in what they need to do.

00:26:48.785 --> 00:26:51.401
(light piano music)

00:26:51.401 --> 00:26:54.449
Could we pull off a Europa
mission to the sub-surface ocean?

00:26:54.449 --> 00:26:56.130
The answer is absolutely yes,

00:26:56.130 --> 00:26:57.353
one hundred percent, yes.

00:26:57.353 --> 00:27:01.230
I believe that it is
launchable within 10 years,

00:27:01.230 --> 00:27:03.570
if the money was on the table today.

00:27:03.570 --> 00:27:04.939
There's two things I think that drive us.

00:27:04.939 --> 00:27:06.679
One is the pursuit of happiness,

00:27:06.679 --> 00:27:10.596
and the second one is
the pursuit of curiosity.

00:27:14.334 --> 00:27:19.194
If Galileo had not been interested
in the motions of planets

00:27:19.194 --> 00:27:21.403
or Copernicus, or Kepler,

00:27:21.403 --> 00:27:23.360
who would have provided the information

00:27:23.360 --> 00:27:28.050
that allowed Newton to figure
out the law of gravitation?

00:27:28.050 --> 00:27:32.167
These little things are
revelations about the universe,

00:27:32.167 --> 00:27:33.894
about us, our place in it.

00:27:33.894 --> 00:27:37.386
How could you not find satisfaction in

00:27:37.386 --> 00:27:39.969
solving those curious problems?

00:27:48.126 --> 00:27:50.262
(soft echoing organ music)

00:27:50.262 --> 00:27:53.762
- To play the instrument that is utilizing

00:27:55.242 --> 00:27:56.659
our gentle Earth,

00:27:57.498 --> 00:28:01.183
it's like, you're becoming
one with the instrument

00:28:01.183 --> 00:28:02.766
in a very true way.

00:28:03.738 --> 00:28:05.821
You just sit and go, yes.

00:28:12.582 --> 00:28:15.582
We're looking at The
Great Stalacpipe Organ

00:28:15.582 --> 00:28:19.399
of the Luray Caverns in Luray, Virginia.

00:28:19.399 --> 00:28:21.523
When I press a key,

00:28:21.523 --> 00:28:25.938
it sends an electrical pulse
up to a rubber tipped mallet,

00:28:25.938 --> 00:28:28.110
which strikes the stalactite,

00:28:28.110 --> 00:28:29.995
causing it to vibrate, and producing a

00:28:29.995 --> 00:28:32.828
incredibly beautiful musical tone.

00:28:34.531 --> 00:28:37.088
The area that the strikers cover

00:28:37.088 --> 00:28:40.555
is roughly three and half acres,

00:28:40.555 --> 00:28:42.498
which then makes it literally,

00:28:42.498 --> 00:28:45.748
the world's largest musical instrument.

00:28:47.947 --> 00:28:50.814
It is a very gentle sound,

00:28:50.814 --> 00:28:51.981
um, very peaceful.

00:28:53.250 --> 00:28:55.313
There's only one like it in the world

00:28:55.313 --> 00:28:59.164
and it's something incredibly different,

00:28:59.164 --> 00:29:01.331
and wonderfully beautiful.

00:29:08.667 --> 00:29:12.834
(peaceful piano music and nature sounds)

00:29:17.074 --> 00:29:18.142
- [Narrator] They look like something

00:29:18.142 --> 00:29:21.001
out of an Indiana Jones movie,

00:29:21.001 --> 00:29:23.751
bridges made out of living trees.

00:29:26.482 --> 00:29:27.899
This is Nongriat,

00:29:29.123 --> 00:29:31.535
a remote village in Northeastern India,

00:29:31.535 --> 00:29:33.132
where locals have been growing bridges

00:29:33.132 --> 00:29:34.965
for hundreds of years.

00:29:41.448 --> 00:29:44.641
This part of the world is
one of the wettest on Earth.

00:29:44.641 --> 00:29:47.083
The trees these bridges are made from

00:29:47.083 --> 00:29:49.820
thrive in the thick tropical forest,

00:29:49.820 --> 00:29:52.307
and do not rot in the rain,

00:29:52.307 --> 00:29:56.140
making them strong enough
to last generations.

00:30:00.838 --> 00:30:04.367
That's Wiston Mua, the
head of the village.

00:30:04.367 --> 00:30:07.950
He's lived here his
whole life, since 1952.

00:30:34.530 --> 00:30:38.001
To get the roots to grow in
a bridge across the water,

00:30:38.001 --> 00:30:42.168
they use wire and old tree
trunks to guide their path.

00:31:32.137 --> 00:31:35.554
(peaceful wintery music)

00:31:37.958 --> 00:31:40.373
Snow means something
different to everyone.

00:31:40.373 --> 00:31:43.442
- Snow takes all the complicated details

00:31:43.442 --> 00:31:45.134
and simplifies the landscape,

00:31:45.134 --> 00:31:48.964
to the possibilities of a
naturally occurring sculpture.

00:31:48.964 --> 00:31:50.515
- [Narrator] For snow globe artists

00:31:50.515 --> 00:31:52.544
Walter Martin and Paloma Muñoz,

00:31:52.544 --> 00:31:56.688
snow is what draws you
into their surreal world.

00:31:56.688 --> 00:31:58.919
- There's like a paradise feeling

00:31:58.919 --> 00:32:00.972
where man and beast live in peace

00:32:00.972 --> 00:32:04.067
and work together to do the laundry.

00:32:04.067 --> 00:32:05.530
- [Narrator] Here, they work out ambitions

00:32:05.530 --> 00:32:07.607
and exercise anxiety.

00:32:07.607 --> 00:32:09.959
- Teetering, there's a sense of teetering

00:32:09.959 --> 00:32:12.311
and of tenuous balance.

00:32:12.311 --> 00:32:14.111
- [Narrator] The globes
freeze miniature landscapes

00:32:14.111 --> 00:32:16.115
in wintery wonder.

00:32:16.115 --> 00:32:18.782
Twisted logic and macabre humor.

00:32:20.207 --> 00:32:22.860
- It's like our own little form of poetry.

00:32:22.860 --> 00:32:25.139
- [Narrator] Inspired by
nature and the elements.

00:32:25.139 --> 00:32:29.306
- We feel a lot of angst and
dread sometimes in the winter,

00:32:31.104 --> 00:32:33.925
and it goes into the globe.

00:32:33.925 --> 00:32:35.386
- [Narrator] The couple
puts a modern twist

00:32:35.386 --> 00:32:37.487
on an age old craft,

00:32:37.487 --> 00:32:41.154
transforming knickknacks 
into custom works of art.

00:32:45.727 --> 00:32:48.524
- So, imagine a world full of stories

00:32:48.524 --> 00:32:50.432
and the camera goes in

00:32:50.432 --> 00:32:53.347
and just picks up this
little fragment in time,

00:32:53.347 --> 00:32:55.303
and that little moment,

00:32:55.303 --> 00:32:56.935
and that's what the globes are.

00:32:56.935 --> 00:32:59.852
They are samples of a larger world,

00:33:00.918 --> 00:33:03.918
but becomes alive when you shake it.

00:33:14.815 --> 00:33:17.924
Working together, for
us, is one experiment,

00:33:17.924 --> 00:33:20.696
maybe like marriage, but it's
a more complex marriage,

00:33:20.696 --> 00:33:23.815
because we're trying
to create art together.

00:33:23.815 --> 00:33:27.883
We've been doing the snow
globes now for almost 15 years.

00:33:27.883 --> 00:33:30.965
(upbeat cheery piano music)

00:33:30.965 --> 00:33:32.471
It was started in 2001.

00:33:32.471 --> 00:33:34.127
That was when we made the first one

00:33:34.127 --> 00:33:36.294
and we had no idea we were

00:33:37.548 --> 00:33:41.381
going to be making snow
globes 15 years later.

00:33:52.969 --> 00:33:55.188
Everything starts with a small canvas that

00:33:55.188 --> 00:33:59.425
is the space that you
have to fill in the globe,

00:33:59.425 --> 00:34:02.665
and then you start adding figures,

00:34:02.665 --> 00:34:04.994
and you continue to explore the landscape

00:34:04.994 --> 00:34:07.968
in relationship to those figures.

00:34:07.968 --> 00:34:10.272
- I think I was fascinated
with the way that

00:34:10.272 --> 00:34:13.897
snow kind of takes all
the complicated details

00:34:13.897 --> 00:34:15.913
and simplifies the landscape,

00:34:15.913 --> 00:34:18.253
the unification of the landscape.

00:34:18.253 --> 00:34:21.237
It tends to be a great equalizer,

00:34:21.237 --> 00:34:23.278
and when there's something
outside of that world

00:34:23.278 --> 00:34:25.673
that's interjected into it, like a human,

00:34:25.673 --> 00:34:29.514
with a colorful wardrobe, or an animal,

00:34:29.514 --> 00:34:33.101
or a small house, or anything
that's not of that world,

00:34:33.101 --> 00:34:35.238
becomes the focus.

00:34:35.238 --> 00:34:36.988
It is a frozen world.

00:34:42.970 --> 00:34:46.390
- [Paloma] We live at
the edge of a small town

00:34:46.390 --> 00:34:49.149
and on the other side of our house is

00:34:49.149 --> 00:34:52.054
a Northeastern American forest.

00:34:52.054 --> 00:34:54.441
It's complete wilderness,

00:34:54.441 --> 00:34:56.373
and we walk it every day,

00:34:56.373 --> 00:34:58.305
and we love it.

00:34:58.305 --> 00:35:00.550
- [Walter] Depending on the
day and the time of year,

00:35:00.550 --> 00:35:02.805
and the time of day, and the weather,

00:35:02.805 --> 00:35:06.598
the forest can offer a
totally different face.

00:35:06.598 --> 00:35:08.722
- It's like we have one
foot in the wilderness

00:35:08.722 --> 00:35:11.250
and one foot on civilization,

00:35:11.250 --> 00:35:14.563
we can bring those worlds
together through our work.

00:35:14.563 --> 00:35:17.980
(mystical wintery music)

00:35:19.938 --> 00:35:22.398
We say a lot of silly
things throughout the day.

00:35:22.398 --> 00:35:24.481
It litters into the work.

00:35:33.378 --> 00:35:34.926
- I'm from Norfolk Virginia,

00:35:34.926 --> 00:35:38.791
which is a flat coastal city
in the mid-Atlantic region,

00:35:38.791 --> 00:35:41.414
not known for its snow storms.

00:35:41.414 --> 00:35:44.691
So, I fantasized about escaping to a place

00:35:44.691 --> 00:35:46.322
where there was snow.

00:35:46.322 --> 00:35:48.099
- For me, the challenge is always,

00:35:48.099 --> 00:35:50.980
“How do I make something that is relevant?”

00:35:50.980 --> 00:35:55.408
That contributes something
to the conversation

00:35:55.408 --> 00:35:59.839
of what art is, and what being
a human being in this moment

00:35:59.839 --> 00:36:01.172
in time is like.

00:36:03.366 --> 00:36:06.777
- [Walter] A successful snow
globe would be absurdist humor,

00:36:06.777 --> 00:36:09.860
combined with a real human condition.

00:36:11.064 --> 00:36:13.453
- I think people find their
globes very interesting,

00:36:13.453 --> 00:36:14.532
they love them.

00:36:14.532 --> 00:36:18.699
They absorb you and they allow
the viewer to be immersed,

00:36:19.680 --> 00:36:23.847
even for just a few seconds in
a completely different world.

00:36:35.595 --> 00:36:38.678
(somber piano music)

00:36:41.015 --> 00:36:43.080
- My name's Tim Doucette.

00:36:43.080 --> 00:36:45.623
I'm an amateur astronomer,

00:36:45.623 --> 00:36:47.918
and I'm also legally blind.

00:36:47.918 --> 00:36:50.335
Kind of an oxymoron, isn't it?

00:36:53.206 --> 00:36:55.751
It turns out, I can
actually see the night sky

00:36:55.751 --> 00:37:00.125
better than most people,
probably better than you.

00:37:00.125 --> 00:37:03.208
(echoing wavy tones)

00:37:12.714 --> 00:37:14.441
When I was younger,

00:37:14.441 --> 00:37:17.250
it's easy to become discouraged.

00:37:17.250 --> 00:37:20.514
You know, realizing that
you're never gonna drive a car,

00:37:20.514 --> 00:37:23.347
that you'll never be an astronaut.

00:37:24.448 --> 00:37:27.198
At least that's what you're told.

00:37:30.629 --> 00:37:33.712
I was born with congenital cataracts.

00:37:34.901 --> 00:37:36.977
They removed the lenses of my eyes

00:37:36.977 --> 00:37:39.089
and widened my pupils.

00:37:39.089 --> 00:37:42.377
That left me with only
about ten percent of my eyesight.

00:37:42.377 --> 00:37:45.900
For the average person, their
pupil automatically adjusts

00:37:45.900 --> 00:37:49.066
for the amount of light coming
in, it opens and closes,

00:37:49.066 --> 00:37:51.936
but for me, my pupil is open.

00:37:51.936 --> 00:37:55.353
So, it's always letting in a lot of light.

00:38:00.768 --> 00:38:04.935
During the day, I see
everything extremely bright.

00:38:06.565 --> 00:38:08.784
Everything is overexposed.

00:38:08.784 --> 00:38:10.617
Colors are more vivid,

00:38:19.924 --> 00:38:22.010
but at night time, the tables are turned,

00:38:22.010 --> 00:38:24.796
and it's like a curtain has been lifted.

00:38:24.796 --> 00:38:28.296
(mystical wondrous music)

00:38:36.735 --> 00:38:38.919
When I was a teenager, I went
on some additional surgery

00:38:38.919 --> 00:38:40.836
to improve my eyesight.

00:38:42.232 --> 00:38:46.037
I got home from the hospital
and I looked up at the sky

00:38:46.037 --> 00:38:48.107
and at first I thought I was actually

00:38:48.107 --> 00:38:49.091
having a detached retina.

00:38:49.091 --> 00:38:52.023
I was seeing millions of spots of light,

00:38:52.023 --> 00:38:56.703
and realized that, that was
the Milky Way I was looking at,

00:38:56.703 --> 00:38:58.836
and it was phenomenal.

00:38:58.836 --> 00:39:02.503
(curious inquisitive music)

00:39:05.389 --> 00:39:08.474
At first, I didn't really realize what

00:39:08.474 --> 00:39:09.807
it had given me.

00:39:11.213 --> 00:39:13.325
I took 15 years later

00:39:13.325 --> 00:39:16.575
to realize that what I had was special.

00:39:19.409 --> 00:39:22.961
With my personal savings, I
built an observatory on a hill

00:39:22.961 --> 00:39:26.461
near my house, and called it Deep Sky Eye.

00:39:30.862 --> 00:39:32.384
When I'm looking through a telescope,

00:39:32.384 --> 00:39:34.508
I'm not wearing my glasses,

00:39:34.508 --> 00:39:38.204
and my eye is like a
camera without a lens.

00:39:38.204 --> 00:39:42.933
So, it's focusing the light
very clearly onto my retina.

00:39:42.933 --> 00:39:44.901
I see a little bit extra light

00:39:44.901 --> 00:39:48.200
that most people wouldn't see.

00:39:48.200 --> 00:39:49.808
You know, you look at the Orion nebula

00:39:49.808 --> 00:39:52.554
and you know, it looks
like a fuzzy patch but,

00:39:52.554 --> 00:39:54.287
you know, I kind of get a little bit of

00:39:54.287 --> 00:39:56.832
purplish tinge to it.

00:39:56.832 --> 00:40:00.999
The sky here is like a
tapestry of interstellar dust.

00:40:03.230 --> 00:40:05.737
It looks like a velvet background,

00:40:05.737 --> 00:40:09.121
you know, with diamonds all through it.

00:40:09.121 --> 00:40:11.121
It's absolutely amazing.

00:40:18.446 --> 00:40:20.946
My wife is also legally blind.

00:40:24.408 --> 00:40:25.945
She doesn't see very well at night,

00:40:25.945 --> 00:40:28.082
but most of the time when
we're, you know, walking,

00:40:28.082 --> 00:40:29.329
or at the observatory,

00:40:29.329 --> 00:40:32.662
I'll explain to my wife what I'm seeing.

00:40:34.596 --> 00:40:36.732
She appreciates the night sky

00:40:36.732 --> 00:40:40.149
even though she doesn't see it very well.

00:40:44.808 --> 00:40:45.963
When I look through the telescope

00:40:45.963 --> 00:40:48.064
or look up at the night sky,

00:40:48.064 --> 00:40:50.477
it makes you realize really
that all your problems

00:40:50.477 --> 00:40:53.167
or the fact that you're
legally blind, or whatever,

00:40:53.167 --> 00:40:55.667
it really just doesn't matter.

00:40:56.763 --> 00:41:00.763
You realize that you're
a part of this universe.

00:41:04.323 --> 00:41:06.906
(echoing bing)

